WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.980 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:03.465 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.465 --> 00:00:05.445 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.445 --> 00:00:10.973 align:middle line:90%


00:00:10.973 --> 00:00:12.140 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: All right.

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:16.960 align:middle line:84%
So today's topic is
stochastic calculus.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.560 align:middle line:84%
And this topic is
really an engaging one

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:28.960 align:middle line:84%
in quantitative finance because
it extends ordinary calculus

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.759 align:middle line:84%
with real variables
to calculus that

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:36.120 align:middle line:84%
depends upon
stochastic processes,

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.120 align:middle line:84%
and in particular,
Brownian motion processes.

00:00:40.120 --> 00:00:42.160 align:middle line:84%
And so when we are
thinking about how

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:48.840 align:middle line:84%
to model the value of a
stock or of a derivative,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.480 align:middle line:84%
such as a simple option
or a more complex option,

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:59.320 align:middle line:84%
we can model the
dynamics of these assets

00:00:59.320 --> 00:01:05.319 align:middle line:84%
or derivative
contracts as integrals

00:01:05.319 --> 00:01:09.320 align:middle line:84%
of functions with respect
to Brownian motion,

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.960 align:middle line:84%
where Brownian motion
characterizes the underlying

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.720 align:middle line:90%
dynamics of the asset.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:18.200 align:middle line:84%
And so it's really
neat to see how

00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.960 align:middle line:84%
we can develop
stochastic calculus

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.760 align:middle line:84%
and relate it to
what's familiar to us

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:29.280 align:middle line:84%
with ordinary real calculus,
but see how there are extensions

00:01:29.280 --> 00:01:37.000 align:middle line:84%
of the formulas that accommodate
the randomness in the increments

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:39.340 align:middle line:90%
with which we're integrating.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:44.160 align:middle line:84%
So let's consider
just reviewing,

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:48.520 align:middle line:84%
first, Brownian
motion with drift.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.320 align:middle line:84%
We discussed this
earlier in our discussion

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:54.400 align:middle line:90%
of stochastic processes.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.990 align:middle line:84%
And a standard
Brownian motion model

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:07.790 align:middle line:84%
is one where we have our process
X, depending upon the underlying

00:02:07.790 --> 00:02:09.509 align:middle line:90%
Brownian motion--

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:17.270 align:middle line:84%
standardized Brownian motion B
of t, with a drift parameter mu,

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:22.670 align:middle line:84%
and a volatility parameter
sigma, or variance parameter

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.430 align:middle line:90%
sigma squared.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:29.406 align:middle line:84%
And the key property
of Brownian motion

00:02:29.406 --> 00:02:35.550 align:middle line:84%
includes independent
increments and the variation

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.950 align:middle line:84%
of increments of the
process being proportional

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.510 align:middle line:90%
to the length of the increment.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.810 align:middle line:84%
So with standard
Brownian motion,

00:02:45.810 --> 00:02:49.670 align:middle line:84%
where we have a
drift parameter of 0

00:02:49.670 --> 00:02:57.750 align:middle line:84%
and a sigma volatility of 1, the
extension of these properties

00:02:57.750 --> 00:03:02.590 align:middle line:84%
to have essentially mean
0 and standard deviation

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:07.230 align:middle line:84%
equal to sigma
times t is to have

00:03:07.230 --> 00:03:10.750 align:middle line:84%
the mean depend upon
time, with the drift

00:03:10.750 --> 00:03:15.430 align:middle line:84%
parameter and the
volatility or variance

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.910 align:middle line:84%
to be proportional
to sigma squared

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:21.070 align:middle line:84%
times the length
of the interval.

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:28.550 align:middle line:84%
Now, in terms of
understanding Brownian motion,

00:03:28.550 --> 00:03:31.710 align:middle line:84%
it's going to be
useful for us to focus

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:38.950 align:middle line:84%
on the conditional distribution
of the value at time

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:53.670 align:middle line:84%
t plus delta as a function
of the value at time X of t.

00:03:53.670 --> 00:03:59.090 align:middle line:84%
And so when we think of a
graph of Brownian motion,

00:03:59.090 --> 00:04:09.110 align:middle line:84%
which is some path, if we
fix, at a given value t,

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:14.710 align:middle line:90%
the value of X of t--

00:04:14.710 --> 00:04:17.340 align:middle line:84%
or maybe I should
write this as s--

00:04:17.340 --> 00:04:20.990 align:middle line:90%


00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:29.470 align:middle line:84%
the value of X at time little
t, then if we know the value

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:33.570 align:middle line:84%
at time little t, what
is the distribution,

00:04:33.570 --> 00:04:36.310 align:middle line:84%
the conditional distribution
into the future?

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:44.030 align:middle line:84%
Well, this increment t plus
delta t, delta t in red,

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:49.870 align:middle line:84%
we can just expand that out to
mu t plus delta t plus sigma

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.750 align:middle line:90%
B of t plus delta t.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:05:00.603 align:middle line:84%
And then we can group the known
terms on the left-hand side,

00:05:00.603 --> 00:05:06.030 align:middle line:84%
or the left-hand terms of the
second equation, mu t plus sigma

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:07.310 align:middle line:90%
B of t.

00:05:07.310 --> 00:05:15.270 align:middle line:84%
Then we have a term mu delta t,
which is a constant, not random.

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:20.470 align:middle line:84%
But then we have the increment
of the Brownian motion

00:05:20.470 --> 00:05:28.390 align:middle line:84%
process from time t to time
delta t, t plus delta t.

00:05:28.390 --> 00:05:30.290 align:middle line:84%
So actually,
there's a typo here.

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:35.510 align:middle line:84%
That should be B of t
plus delta t minus B of t.

00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:43.110 align:middle line:84%
And so we have that our
delta X increment is mu delta

00:05:43.110 --> 00:05:45.590 align:middle line:90%
t plus sigma delta B of t.

00:05:45.590 --> 00:05:50.470 align:middle line:84%
Now, this increment
has mean mu delta t,

00:05:50.470 --> 00:05:52.340 align:middle line:90%
variance sigma squared delta t.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:57.220 align:middle line:84%
And for Brownian
motion with drift,

00:05:57.220 --> 00:06:01.100 align:middle line:84%
this increment is
exactly distributed

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:06.540 align:middle line:84%
as a normal random variable,
with that mean and variance.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:12.180 align:middle line:84%
And what's useful
to think about is,

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:19.940 align:middle line:84%
what happens as the increment
delta t gets infinitesimal?

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:26.620 align:middle line:84%
And so if we look at the
infinitesimal increment

00:06:26.620 --> 00:06:32.900 align:middle line:84%
for delta t, then the
expected squared value

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:37.380 align:middle line:84%
of the Brownian
motion increment is

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:43.200 align:middle line:84%
equal to sigma squared delta
t plus mu delta t squared.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.780 align:middle line:84%
This is actually
an exact formula.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:50.100 align:middle line:84%
But what's important for
us is that, as delta t goes

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:53.260 align:middle line:84%
to 0, as it becomes
infinitesimal,

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:59.620 align:middle line:84%
then this squared
increment has expectation

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:03.820 align:middle line:90%
that's of order delta t.

00:07:03.820 --> 00:07:06.620 align:middle line:84%
And it actually equals sigma
squared delta t plus little

00:07:06.620 --> 00:07:09.220 align:middle line:90%
o of delta t.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:14.300 align:middle line:84%
So this little o notation means
that as delta t goes to 0,

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:18.980 align:middle line:84%
this remainder part
goes to 0 as well.

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:24.900 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider powers
of our increment of delta

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:30.660 align:middle line:84%
X, where the power
c is greater than 2,

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:35.900 align:middle line:84%
then this will also
be small o of delta t.

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:44.340 align:middle line:84%
So expressions that depend
upon second or higher order

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:48.660 align:middle line:84%
of delta X will actually
be comparable to sigma

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:49.800 align:middle line:90%
squared delta t.

00:07:49.800 --> 00:07:52.660 align:middle line:90%


00:07:52.660 --> 00:07:57.220 align:middle line:84%
So with Brownian
motion and drift,

00:07:57.220 --> 00:08:01.020 align:middle line:84%
we have the notation for
a stochastic differential

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:05.700 align:middle line:84%
equation, which is
simply the increment

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:16.400 align:middle line:84%
of X is equal to mu delta t, or
dt rather, plus sigma dB of t.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:20.860 align:middle line:90%


00:08:20.860 --> 00:08:28.340 align:middle line:84%
And with this
notation, the solution

00:08:28.340 --> 00:08:35.340 align:middle line:84%
turns out to be X of t is equal
to the value of X at time 0

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:40.940 align:middle line:84%
plus the integral from 0 to t
mu dt plus the integral from 0

00:08:40.940 --> 00:08:43.509 align:middle line:90%
to t of sigma dBs.

00:08:43.509 --> 00:08:46.370 align:middle line:90%


00:08:46.370 --> 00:08:54.250 align:middle line:84%
And this solution
seems obvious when

00:08:54.250 --> 00:09:00.610 align:middle line:84%
we think about how we understand
differential equations

00:09:00.610 --> 00:09:05.170 align:middle line:84%
with real functions, but it
does extend to this solution

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:07.850 align:middle line:90%
when we have Brownian motion.

00:09:07.850 --> 00:09:14.730 align:middle line:84%
And so as noted there,
this is equal to X0 plus t

00:09:14.730 --> 00:09:24.490 align:middle line:84%
minus 0 times mu plus sigma
times B of t minus B of 0.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:32.130 align:middle line:84%
So our integration of
functions of Brownian motion

00:09:32.130 --> 00:09:36.810 align:middle line:84%
have this nominal solution,
which seems very reasonable.

00:09:36.810 --> 00:09:43.730 align:middle line:84%
And we'll, in fact, show
that this solution is useful

00:09:43.730 --> 00:09:50.050 align:middle line:84%
and it actually extends
to more complex equations.

00:09:50.050 --> 00:09:54.530 align:middle line:84%
Now, the more complex
differential equations

00:09:54.530 --> 00:09:59.510 align:middle line:84%
are one where we consider
a drift term, mu,

00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:01.850 align:middle line:90%
that's not a constant.

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:05.730 align:middle line:84%
It may vary over
time, and it may also

00:10:05.730 --> 00:10:11.490 align:middle line:84%
vary depending upon the
magnitude of the process.

00:10:11.490 --> 00:10:17.370 align:middle line:84%
So this kind of an
extension is quite general.

00:10:17.370 --> 00:10:22.370 align:middle line:84%
And we can think about the same
extension to the volatility.

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:25.290 align:middle line:84%
Rather than being a constant,
it might vary with t,

00:10:25.290 --> 00:10:28.290 align:middle line:90%
and it might vary with Xt.

00:10:28.290 --> 00:10:36.330 align:middle line:84%
And with derivative
contracts, we

00:10:36.330 --> 00:10:44.450 align:middle line:84%
have values of contracts
which, at any given time,

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:49.130 align:middle line:84%
depend on time, maybe
time to maturity,

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:52.530 align:middle line:90%
and the level of the process.

00:10:52.530 --> 00:10:56.970 align:middle line:84%
And apart from
those two features

00:10:56.970 --> 00:11:01.810 align:middle line:84%
or properties of the
derivative at time little t,

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:06.650 align:middle line:84%
we have a valuation
model that applies,

00:11:06.650 --> 00:11:08.650 align:middle line:90%
according to some function.

00:11:08.650 --> 00:11:10.770 align:middle line:84%
So it will be very
useful to have

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:14.850 align:middle line:84%
these generalized drift
and generalized volatility

00:11:14.850 --> 00:11:15.690 align:middle line:90%
parameters.

00:11:15.690 --> 00:11:17.210 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:20.130 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How is the
second integral defined?

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:25.090 align:middle line:84%
Because, obviously, the first
one is like a Riemann integral.

00:11:25.090 --> 00:11:27.090 align:middle line:90%
What about the second one?

00:11:27.090 --> 00:11:29.310 align:middle line:84%
Is there an actual
formal definition of--

00:11:29.310 --> 00:11:30.670 align:middle line:90%
I guess it's an Ito integral.

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:31.670 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: Right.

00:11:31.670 --> 00:11:34.430 align:middle line:84%
And we will get
there in a moment.

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:38.570 align:middle line:90%
But if we're integrating from--

00:11:38.570 --> 00:11:41.810 align:middle line:90%
well, here it's from 0 to t.

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:46.970 align:middle line:84%
If we're integrating the
process over increments,

00:11:46.970 --> 00:11:50.530 align:middle line:84%
we're basically going to be
summing over the increments,

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:54.410 align:middle line:84%
although summing on the
infinitesimal timescale

00:11:54.410 --> 00:11:58.050 align:middle line:84%
over the entire period
between 0 and t.

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:03.290 align:middle line:84%
And so if we break that sum
of infinitesimal increments,

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:06.270 align:middle line:84%
we basically get the last
value minus the first.

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:09.330 align:middle line:84%
And so we'll see
that come into play.

00:12:09.330 --> 00:12:14.730 align:middle line:84%
So what we will determine
with this general setup,

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:20.170 align:middle line:84%
though, is that when we have
extended drift and volatility,

00:12:20.170 --> 00:12:24.210 align:middle line:84%
we then will have a solution
to this Brownian motion

00:12:24.210 --> 00:12:29.370 align:middle line:84%
with drift, with generalized
drift and volatility, satisfying

00:12:29.370 --> 00:12:30.970 align:middle line:90%
this equation.

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:37.410 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to want to be
able to compute these integrals

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:39.030 align:middle line:90%
in a consistent way.

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:42.440 align:middle line:84%
And the Ito calculus
provides us with that.

00:12:42.440 --> 00:12:47.040 align:middle line:84%
So we have to define
Ito integrals.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:53.160 align:middle line:84%
Well, in defining
Ito integrals, we

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:58.640 align:middle line:84%
have this notation that we
need to be comfortable with.

00:12:58.640 --> 00:13:07.800 align:middle line:84%
We are going to, first of all,
consider a probability process

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:13.000 align:middle line:90%
that is defined by this triple.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:14.260 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:23.240 align:middle line:84%
Have any people here taken
advanced probability or measure

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:25.760 align:middle line:90%
theory?

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:32.960 align:middle line:84%
Well, we basically have,
here, a sample space.

00:13:32.960 --> 00:13:40.880 align:middle line:84%
And here, we have what's
called a sigma field of sets,

00:13:40.880 --> 00:13:43.880 align:middle line:90%
which are a subset of omega.

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:49.640 align:middle line:84%
And then here, we have
a probability model,

00:13:49.640 --> 00:13:55.880 align:middle line:84%
where if we have A,
a subset of omega,

00:13:55.880 --> 00:14:00.240 align:middle line:84%
and it's an element
of this sigma field,

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:06.250 align:middle line:84%
we have this probability of
that set is well defined.

00:14:06.250 --> 00:14:08.760 align:middle line:90%


00:14:08.760 --> 00:14:16.760 align:middle line:84%
So this notation encompasses the
generality of probability models

00:14:16.760 --> 00:14:23.520 align:middle line:84%
that are important to specify
and be comfortable with.

00:14:23.520 --> 00:14:32.800 align:middle line:84%
Well, with the omega
set here, these

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:36.440 align:middle line:84%
are a collection
of little omegas,

00:14:36.440 --> 00:14:49.880 align:middle line:84%
which are possible sample
paths of this Brownian motion.

00:14:49.880 --> 00:15:00.960 align:middle line:84%
And we can think of, OK, here's
one path, one possible path.

00:15:00.960 --> 00:15:06.960 align:middle line:84%
Another possible path actually
matches the first path up

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:13.320 align:middle line:84%
to time t, but then
continues on somewhere else.

00:15:13.320 --> 00:15:18.940 align:middle line:84%
Another path could
be one that is

00:15:18.940 --> 00:15:22.460 align:middle line:90%
reflected about a given level.

00:15:22.460 --> 00:15:24.400 align:middle line:84%
We went through this a
bit in our discussion

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:27.280 align:middle line:90%
of Brownian motion processes.

00:15:27.280 --> 00:15:30.200 align:middle line:84%
And so we can think
of just the collection

00:15:30.200 --> 00:15:32.520 align:middle line:90%
of all possible paths.

00:15:32.520 --> 00:15:37.830 align:middle line:90%
Now, the filtration, Ft--

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:42.790 align:middle line:84%
we think of script
Ft, this is going

00:15:42.790 --> 00:15:53.310 align:middle line:84%
to be the sigma field of
events or paths, which

00:15:53.310 --> 00:16:06.630 align:middle line:84%
are fixed or known at times
s less than or equal to t.

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:13.230 align:middle line:84%
So with script Ft, the
information up to time t--

00:16:13.230 --> 00:16:22.030 align:middle line:84%
if this is our
path up to time t,

00:16:22.030 --> 00:16:28.270 align:middle line:84%
the event of following
this path up to time t

00:16:28.270 --> 00:16:34.150 align:middle line:84%
is deterministic
if we look back--

00:16:34.150 --> 00:16:38.470 align:middle line:84%
deterministic for
s less than t--

00:16:38.470 --> 00:16:41.795 align:middle line:90%
but then it's random--

00:16:41.795 --> 00:16:46.390 align:middle line:90%
random-- for s greater than t.

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:51.550 align:middle line:90%
So we have a value--

00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:53.970 align:middle line:84%
or in script Ft,
it basically says,

00:16:53.970 --> 00:17:01.870 align:middle line:84%
OK, we can work with paths that
are deterministic before time t

00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:05.470 align:middle line:84%
but then are random
after time t.

00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:13.109 align:middle line:84%
And so that relates to the
concept of being measurable.

00:17:13.109 --> 00:17:17.430 align:middle line:84%
So we say that events in Ft
are measurable with respect

00:17:17.430 --> 00:17:21.790 align:middle line:84%
to Bs, with the information
set up to time t.

00:17:21.790 --> 00:17:27.470 align:middle line:84%
So if we consider
times before t,

00:17:27.470 --> 00:17:32.150 align:middle line:84%
the path is actually
just a constant--

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:37.470 align:middle line:84%
or as a function, a path-valued
function that's constant.

00:17:37.470 --> 00:17:43.270 align:middle line:84%
Now, if we have this
probability measure

00:17:43.270 --> 00:17:48.710 align:middle line:84%
on omega, the space of
all possible paths--

00:17:48.710 --> 00:17:55.390 align:middle line:84%
if we consider these a
conditional distribution given

00:17:55.390 --> 00:18:00.150 align:middle line:84%
X at time t, then
we're trying to specify

00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:07.230 align:middle line:84%
the model for all future paths
that start at the level X

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:08.830 align:middle line:90%
at time t.

00:18:08.830 --> 00:18:21.510 align:middle line:84%
And that probability measure
over the entire space of paths

00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:26.910 align:middle line:84%
can also be restricted to the
paths up to time little t.

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:34.910 align:middle line:84%
So we can basically focus
on probability measures that

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:41.030 align:middle line:84%
are unconditional but restricted
to the information set up

00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:42.670 align:middle line:90%
to time t.

00:18:42.670 --> 00:18:48.870 align:middle line:84%
Now, we'll want to refer to the
value of the Brownian motion

00:18:48.870 --> 00:18:50.750 align:middle line:90%
at time t.

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:58.990 align:middle line:84%
And it's important to know that
often this notation, Bt, is

00:18:58.990 --> 00:19:03.710 align:middle line:84%
implicitly the value
of a specific path.

00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:06.990 align:middle line:84%
So to emphasize
that, we'll indicate

00:19:06.990 --> 00:19:15.030 align:middle line:84%
that it's the Brownian motion
path indexed by little omega.

00:19:15.030 --> 00:19:23.510 align:middle line:84%
And so we then want to consider
the Ito integrals with respect

00:19:23.510 --> 00:19:28.030 align:middle line:84%
to Brownian motion,
first where we consider

00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:36.140 align:middle line:84%
simple functions, real
functions, where f of Bs

00:19:36.140 --> 00:19:42.140 align:middle line:84%
is the value of little f
evaluated at the position

00:19:42.140 --> 00:19:45.660 align:middle line:90%
value of the Brownian motion.

00:19:45.660 --> 00:19:49.220 align:middle line:84%
And we'll want to
consider integrating

00:19:49.220 --> 00:20:00.320 align:middle line:84%
that function from 0 to T.
And so this is our notation.

00:20:00.320 --> 00:20:15.460 align:middle line:84%
So with a companion graph,
with the same x-axis

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:20.940 align:middle line:84%
as our Brownian motion,
we could have f being

00:20:20.940 --> 00:20:27.520 align:middle line:90%
some function which varies.

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:31.260 align:middle line:84%
And we want to
basically integrate

00:20:31.260 --> 00:20:36.560 align:middle line:84%
this function f evaluated
at the Brownian motion.

00:20:36.560 --> 00:20:43.360 align:middle line:84%
So actually, this is
actually a wrong graph.

00:20:43.360 --> 00:20:46.100 align:middle line:84%
I thought we had
had the same graph.

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:58.700 align:middle line:84%
We basically want for, let's
say, x equals Bs of omega,

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:00.900 align:middle line:90%
some function.

00:21:00.900 --> 00:21:02.860 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:21:02.860 --> 00:21:11.140 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we consider this
notation to represent our Ito

00:21:11.140 --> 00:21:17.060 align:middle line:84%
integral, we're going to develop
the formulas for these Ito

00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:19.300 align:middle line:90%
integrals.

00:21:19.300 --> 00:21:31.500 align:middle line:84%
And importantly, this Ito
integral is going to have,

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:38.060 align:middle line:84%
basically, a deterministic,
simple, real integration

00:21:38.060 --> 00:21:41.740 align:middle line:90%
term for each path.

00:21:41.740 --> 00:21:47.620 align:middle line:84%
But the paths indexed
by omega are random.

00:21:47.620 --> 00:21:50.340 align:middle line:84%
And so this Ito
integral is actually

00:21:50.340 --> 00:21:52.780 align:middle line:90%
going to be a random variable.

00:21:52.780 --> 00:21:55.620 align:middle line:84%
And as a random
variable, if it exists,

00:21:55.620 --> 00:21:58.653 align:middle line:84%
it'll have an expectation
and a variance.

00:21:58.653 --> 00:22:02.140 align:middle line:90%


00:22:02.140 --> 00:22:05.700 align:middle line:84%
So let's consider a
number of special cases

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:10.140 align:middle line:90%
that will define Ito integrals.

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:18.060 align:middle line:84%
The first case is the simple
one, where f of x is equal to 1.

00:22:18.060 --> 00:22:26.260 align:middle line:84%
So let me just erase this
and have the level 1.

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:30.230 align:middle line:84%
So we consider f
of x equaling 1.

00:22:30.230 --> 00:22:32.900 align:middle line:90%


00:22:32.900 --> 00:22:38.100 align:middle line:84%
So if we integrate the
increments of Brownian motion

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:45.180 align:middle line:84%
over from 0 to capital
T, let's propose

00:22:45.180 --> 00:22:54.420 align:middle line:84%
that that's equal to the
increment from 0 to--

00:22:54.420 --> 00:22:58.340 align:middle line:90%
from 0 to t for each path.

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:04.420 align:middle line:84%
So the right-hand side
corresponds to the simple

00:23:04.420 --> 00:23:14.660 align:middle line:84%
integral of this path,
Bs of omega, between Bt--

00:23:14.660 --> 00:23:18.860 align:middle line:90%
between time 0 and capital T.

00:23:18.860 --> 00:23:25.030 align:middle line:84%
And, I guess, it's
not in the notes.

00:23:25.030 --> 00:23:37.330 align:middle line:84%
But if we were to consider the
integral of 0 to t dBs of omega,

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:53.710 align:middle line:84%
we could think of this as the
sum from 0 to n of iT over n.

00:23:53.710 --> 00:23:56.730 align:middle line:90%


00:23:56.730 --> 00:23:57.590 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:23:57.590 --> 00:24:00.690 align:middle line:90%


00:24:00.690 --> 00:24:04.102 align:middle line:84%
Let me do this, if
i equals 1 to n,

00:24:04.102 --> 00:24:08.450 align:middle line:84%
i minus 1 T over
n-- the integral

00:24:08.450 --> 00:24:17.923 align:middle line:84%
from i minus 1 T over n
to i times T over n dBs.

00:24:17.923 --> 00:24:21.130 align:middle line:90%


00:24:21.130 --> 00:24:25.450 align:middle line:84%
So here, I'm thinking
of basically dividing

00:24:25.450 --> 00:24:31.770 align:middle line:84%
this increment from 0 to T
into different increments

00:24:31.770 --> 00:24:34.770 align:middle line:90%
of width capital T over n.

00:24:34.770 --> 00:24:41.210 align:middle line:90%
And if we approximate--

00:24:41.210 --> 00:24:44.890 align:middle line:84%
or each of these integrals
here are basically

00:24:44.890 --> 00:24:51.610 align:middle line:84%
looking at the integral
of or the change in Bs

00:24:51.610 --> 00:24:56.250 align:middle line:84%
from these lower limits
to the upper limits.

00:24:56.250 --> 00:25:03.218 align:middle line:84%
And so the integral
of dBs of omega,

00:25:03.218 --> 00:25:07.390 align:middle line:84%
in a consistent way
or well-defined way,

00:25:07.390 --> 00:25:10.850 align:middle line:84%
is defined by this increment
of the whole process.

00:25:10.850 --> 00:25:15.170 align:middle line:84%
Now, if that's true,
then when we consider

00:25:15.170 --> 00:25:22.210 align:middle line:84%
the randomness in omega, the
expectation of this integral

00:25:22.210 --> 00:25:26.450 align:middle line:84%
is 0, where B is the
standard Brownian motion.

00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:29.170 align:middle line:84%
And its variance is
equal to the length

00:25:29.170 --> 00:25:33.280 align:middle line:84%
of the interval times the sigma
of standard Brownian motion.

00:25:33.280 --> 00:25:35.850 align:middle line:90%


00:25:35.850 --> 00:25:42.530 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we change
our function f

00:25:42.530 --> 00:25:46.610 align:middle line:84%
to be not the constant
1 over the whole

00:25:46.610 --> 00:25:50.890 align:middle line:84%
real line but just the
indicator of an interval,

00:25:50.890 --> 00:25:56.250 align:middle line:84%
there's just one
between a and b.

00:25:56.250 --> 00:25:59.010 align:middle line:90%
So we have a and b here.

00:25:59.010 --> 00:26:02.290 align:middle line:90%
And we have basically 0 up to a.

00:26:02.290 --> 00:26:11.060 align:middle line:84%
And then it equals 1 up to
b, and then it's 0 beyond.

00:26:11.060 --> 00:26:13.810 align:middle line:90%


00:26:13.810 --> 00:26:18.960 align:middle line:84%
Then this Ito integral
is essentially

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:23.160 align:middle line:84%
looking at the change in
Brownian motion from time

00:26:23.160 --> 00:26:25.720 align:middle line:90%
a to time b.

00:26:25.720 --> 00:26:33.120 align:middle line:84%
So no surprise that that's
a reasonable definition.

00:26:33.120 --> 00:26:37.800 align:middle line:84%
Now, from these
reasonable definitions,

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:45.620 align:middle line:84%
we can consider a second case,
where our function f is, again,

00:26:45.620 --> 00:26:54.680 align:middle line:84%
a function only of time s, but
it's given by a step function

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:58.600 align:middle line:90%
over time, with steps--

00:26:58.600 --> 00:27:02.160 align:middle line:84%
or levels given by the
ai's that are constant.

00:27:02.160 --> 00:27:09.706 align:middle line:84%
So we go from this single-step
function here to considering.

00:27:09.706 --> 00:27:13.040 align:middle line:90%


00:27:13.040 --> 00:27:21.040 align:middle line:84%
basically, different step
functions for our function f.

00:27:21.040 --> 00:27:27.640 align:middle line:84%
Now, these step functions could
correspond to, like, the number

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:31.080 align:middle line:90%
of shares held of an asset.

00:27:31.080 --> 00:27:37.720 align:middle line:84%
And so this integral could
correspond to the gain or loss

00:27:37.720 --> 00:27:43.400 align:middle line:84%
if the underlying asset
followed Brownian motion.

00:27:43.400 --> 00:27:47.600 align:middle line:84%
But here, with this
particular step function,

00:27:47.600 --> 00:27:51.160 align:middle line:84%
we can define the
Ito integral to just

00:27:51.160 --> 00:27:55.800 align:middle line:84%
be the sum over the
increments of the step

00:27:55.800 --> 00:27:59.240 align:middle line:84%
levels times the
increment of the process.

00:27:59.240 --> 00:28:05.180 align:middle line:84%
And so, that's a pretty sensible
definition of the integral.

00:28:05.180 --> 00:28:09.760 align:middle line:84%
And it would apply if we
had assets traveling--

00:28:09.760 --> 00:28:12.160 align:middle line:84%
values going by
a Brownian motion

00:28:12.160 --> 00:28:17.280 align:middle line:84%
process and, say, shares
given by the a constants that

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:21.520 align:middle line:90%
were maybe deterministic.

00:28:21.520 --> 00:28:25.520 align:middle line:84%
Now, at this point,
we're using functions

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:31.720 align:middle line:84%
f, which are deterministic
functions of time.

00:28:31.720 --> 00:28:40.160 align:middle line:84%
We can then extend this
to random functions.

00:28:40.160 --> 00:28:42.680 align:middle line:84%
And actually,
before I do so, just

00:28:42.680 --> 00:28:49.520 align:middle line:84%
notice in red, the mean 0
and the variance of this Ito

00:28:49.520 --> 00:28:53.440 align:middle line:90%
integral of the step function--

00:28:53.440 --> 00:28:58.080 align:middle line:84%
the variance is simply
the sum of the squares

00:28:58.080 --> 00:29:04.240 align:middle line:84%
of the levels times the
length of the increment

00:29:04.240 --> 00:29:08.200 align:middle line:90%
over which that level applies.

00:29:08.200 --> 00:29:12.600 align:middle line:84%
So now, case 3, things
get more interesting.

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:19.800 align:middle line:84%
We can allow our levels,
ai, to be random variables.

00:29:19.800 --> 00:29:23.450 align:middle line:84%
And these variables,
random variables ai--

00:29:23.450 --> 00:29:28.200 align:middle line:90%


00:29:28.200 --> 00:29:36.800 align:middle line:84%
so ai equal to ai
omega, this is a path.

00:29:36.800 --> 00:29:40.680 align:middle line:84%
And so we can have the
levels vary depending

00:29:40.680 --> 00:29:47.240 align:middle line:90%
upon what the path value is.

00:29:47.240 --> 00:29:56.180 align:middle line:84%
And so we want the
ai to depend on--

00:29:56.180 --> 00:30:01.680 align:middle line:90%


00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:05.220 align:middle line:84%
and, I guess, by depend,
I'll say be deterministic--

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:08.160 align:middle line:90%


00:30:08.160 --> 00:30:12.030 align:middle line:90%
on script Ft sub i.

00:30:12.030 --> 00:30:21.710 align:middle line:84%
So if we define our
levels to be functions

00:30:21.710 --> 00:30:26.990 align:middle line:84%
that are measurable in
this information set,

00:30:26.990 --> 00:30:30.350 align:middle line:84%
then that's what
we're doing here.

00:30:30.350 --> 00:30:38.710 align:middle line:84%
Now earlier, we talked about
nonanticipatory variables.

00:30:38.710 --> 00:30:41.490 align:middle line:84%
And so this is a
nonanticipatory variable.

00:30:41.490 --> 00:30:46.590 align:middle line:84%
It basically says that
the level from ti on,

00:30:46.590 --> 00:30:49.830 align:middle line:84%
for whatever that
increment is, is

00:30:49.830 --> 00:30:53.750 align:middle line:84%
going to be a function of
what's happened in the past.

00:30:53.750 --> 00:30:59.350 align:middle line:84%
So this is like a
realizable function f,

00:30:59.350 --> 00:31:07.190 align:middle line:84%
where when we apply the f
function to the Brownian motion

00:31:07.190 --> 00:31:13.630 align:middle line:84%
increments, we're applying
known increments at all times.

00:31:13.630 --> 00:31:17.710 align:middle line:84%
And so with this
case, we actually

00:31:17.710 --> 00:31:22.590 align:middle line:84%
want to make an assumption
of measurability

00:31:22.590 --> 00:31:26.590 align:middle line:90%
on that i-th information set.

00:31:26.590 --> 00:31:32.950 align:middle line:84%
And we want a bounded squared
expectation of the level.

00:31:32.950 --> 00:31:36.950 align:middle line:84%
So if we have those
two properties then,

00:31:36.950 --> 00:31:43.430 align:middle line:84%
we can write out the Ito
integral as this formula.

00:31:43.430 --> 00:31:47.630 align:middle line:84%
And it will have
expectation equal to 0

00:31:47.630 --> 00:31:54.590 align:middle line:84%
and variance equal to the sum of
the expected squared ai's times

00:31:54.590 --> 00:31:57.070 align:middle line:90%
ti plus 1 minus ti.

00:31:57.070 --> 00:32:00.670 align:middle line:90%


00:32:00.670 --> 00:32:09.790 align:middle line:84%
So, I guess, rather importantly
with these developments so far,

00:32:09.790 --> 00:32:15.990 align:middle line:84%
we're thinking of the
time s ranging from 0 up

00:32:15.990 --> 00:32:26.070 align:middle line:84%
to time capital T. And we have
increments ti, ti plus 1, where

00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:31.150 align:middle line:90%
this increment is something.

00:32:31.150 --> 00:32:34.730 align:middle line:84%
For simplicity, we could define
this increment to have length,

00:32:34.730 --> 00:32:39.410 align:middle line:84%
say, t over n, for n increments
and have equal increments.

00:32:39.410 --> 00:32:42.230 align:middle line:90%


00:32:42.230 --> 00:32:47.990 align:middle line:84%
But this formula
for the Ito integral

00:32:47.990 --> 00:32:55.190 align:middle line:84%
is considering integrating
the Brownian motion

00:32:55.190 --> 00:33:04.870 align:middle line:84%
over the interval from 0 to T.
So integral from 0 to T, f of s,

00:33:04.870 --> 00:33:06.120 align:middle line:90%
Bs dBs.

00:33:06.120 --> 00:33:09.060 align:middle line:90%


00:33:09.060 --> 00:33:15.100 align:middle line:84%
This is our integral,
our Ito process, I of f.

00:33:15.100 --> 00:33:26.340 align:middle line:84%
And we can write I of f
evaluated at a particular path

00:33:26.340 --> 00:33:27.970 align:middle line:90%
given by this formula.

00:33:27.970 --> 00:33:31.260 align:middle line:90%


00:33:31.260 --> 00:33:33.180 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:33:33.180 --> 00:33:39.980 align:middle line:84%
Well, an interesting example
just from a mathematician's

00:33:39.980 --> 00:33:47.300 align:middle line:84%
standpoint is, what if
we define our levels

00:33:47.300 --> 00:33:52.300 align:middle line:84%
to be equal to the value of
the Brownian motion itself?

00:33:52.300 --> 00:33:58.420 align:middle line:90%
So we're integrating B, dB.

00:33:58.420 --> 00:34:02.670 align:middle line:84%
So we're thinking of
this example, so case 3--

00:34:02.670 --> 00:34:06.420 align:middle line:90%


00:34:06.420 --> 00:34:13.699 align:middle line:84%
special case 3-- integral
from 0 to T of B sub s dBs.

00:34:13.699 --> 00:34:18.860 align:middle line:90%


00:34:18.860 --> 00:34:32.100 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we consider
discretizing this Ito integral

00:34:32.100 --> 00:34:37.100 align:middle line:90%
with these random levels ai--

00:34:37.100 --> 00:34:39.420 align:middle line:84%
actually, I'm getting
ahead of myself.

00:34:39.420 --> 00:34:41.429 align:middle line:84%
This integral will
be a limit of these.

00:34:41.429 --> 00:34:44.179 align:middle line:90%


00:34:44.179 --> 00:34:56.219 align:middle line:84%
For any fixed set of increments
that we apply this random level.

00:34:56.219 --> 00:34:58.340 align:middle line:84%
given by the value of
the Brownian motion

00:34:58.340 --> 00:35:04.740 align:middle line:84%
on those increments, then
for each of these cases

00:35:04.740 --> 00:35:10.180 align:middle line:84%
with n increments, we
get that we have mean 0.

00:35:10.180 --> 00:35:16.260 align:middle line:84%
And the variance is equal to
the sum of the variances of each

00:35:16.260 --> 00:35:17.900 align:middle line:90%
of the terms.

00:35:17.900 --> 00:35:21.140 align:middle line:84%
And that turns out to be equal
to the sum over the increments

00:35:21.140 --> 00:35:28.220 align:middle line:84%
of the time Increment t,
level ti, times the increment

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:33.300 align:middle line:90%
to the next time value.

00:35:33.300 --> 00:35:42.300 align:middle line:84%
And this sum of ti times ti plus
1 minus ti is essentially equal

00:35:42.300 --> 00:35:49.940 align:middle line:84%
or close to the integral
from 0 to capital T of sds,

00:35:49.940 --> 00:35:53.460 align:middle line:90%
which should be 1/2 t squared.

00:35:53.460 --> 00:35:55.380 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:35:55.380 --> 00:35:58.585 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How do you conclude
that the expectation is 0?

00:35:58.585 --> 00:35:59.460 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: OK.

00:35:59.460 --> 00:36:07.300 align:middle line:84%
The expectation is 0 because
each of these increments

00:36:07.300 --> 00:36:12.200 align:middle line:90%
here have expectation 0.

00:36:12.200 --> 00:36:14.460 align:middle line:90%
So we're assuming B of 0 is 0.

00:36:14.460 --> 00:36:16.140 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:17.040 align:middle line:90%
Oh, yeah.

00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:20.640 align:middle line:84%
But what if ai is
not independent?

00:36:20.640 --> 00:36:24.385 align:middle line:84%
Because when you take the
expectations [INAUDIBLE].

00:36:24.385 --> 00:36:25.260 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: OK.

00:36:25.260 --> 00:36:25.540 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:36:25.540 --> 00:36:26.040 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:36:26.040 --> 00:36:29.060 align:middle line:90%


00:36:29.060 --> 00:36:29.640 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:36:29.640 --> 00:36:36.820 align:middle line:84%
OK, ai omega is an
element of F sub ti.

00:36:36.820 --> 00:36:51.500 align:middle line:90%
So it's constant on F sub ti.

00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:56.520 align:middle line:90%
So let's see.

00:36:56.520 --> 00:37:00.380 align:middle line:90%


00:37:00.380 --> 00:37:10.900 align:middle line:90%
So ai of omega and Bti plus 1.

00:37:10.900 --> 00:37:20.810 align:middle line:90%


00:37:20.810 --> 00:37:27.530 align:middle line:84%
This increment into
the future from time ti

00:37:27.530 --> 00:37:31.790 align:middle line:90%
is independent of the past.

00:37:31.790 --> 00:37:39.850 align:middle line:84%
And so its expectation, if we
take the product of these two

00:37:39.850 --> 00:37:53.150 align:middle line:84%
and take expectations, we can
take the expectation given--

00:37:53.150 --> 00:37:55.690 align:middle line:90%


00:37:55.690 --> 00:38:04.370 align:middle line:84%
or the expectation over ti
times the expectation given--

00:38:04.370 --> 00:38:14.890 align:middle line:84%
let's see-- over
ti plus 1 given ti.

00:38:14.890 --> 00:38:17.010 align:middle line:84%
So we can break down
this expectation

00:38:17.010 --> 00:38:19.830 align:middle line:90%
into its decomposition.

00:38:19.830 --> 00:38:22.730 align:middle line:84%
And the expectation
of this factor,

00:38:22.730 --> 00:38:24.395 align:middle line:90%
conditionally, will be 0.

00:38:24.395 --> 00:38:25.770 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah,
that makes sense.

00:38:25.770 --> 00:38:30.090 align:middle line:90%
PETER KEMPTHORNE: So, yep.

00:38:30.090 --> 00:38:32.370 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:38:32.370 --> 00:38:42.530 align:middle line:84%
Well, this case 4 is the
case that essentially defines

00:38:42.530 --> 00:38:49.090 align:middle line:84%
Ito integrals in general,
which is, if we consider taking

00:38:49.090 --> 00:38:54.530 align:middle line:84%
the limit of
integrals with respect

00:38:54.530 --> 00:38:57.930 align:middle line:84%
to step functions
with random levels

00:38:57.930 --> 00:39:03.270 align:middle line:84%
ai, but then allow
the increments--

00:39:03.270 --> 00:39:08.890 align:middle line:84%
the time steps to get closer
and closer to each other,

00:39:08.890 --> 00:39:17.330 align:middle line:84%
then the limiting value of
this Ito integral of the step

00:39:17.330 --> 00:39:22.290 align:middle line:84%
functions is equal to
the Ito integral of f.

00:39:22.290 --> 00:39:35.650 align:middle line:84%
So in our special case where f
of x equals x, f of the Brownian

00:39:35.650 --> 00:39:39.690 align:middle line:84%
motion is the Brownian
motion itself,

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:42.890 align:middle line:90%
we end up getting this formula.

00:39:42.890 --> 00:39:49.890 align:middle line:84%
And here, we have basically
the discretized value

00:39:49.890 --> 00:39:52.250 align:middle line:90%
of the integral.

00:39:52.250 --> 00:39:57.400 align:middle line:84%
And then what's of interest is
to see how the limit of that

00:39:57.400 --> 00:40:01.200 align:middle line:90%
equals this formula.

00:40:01.200 --> 00:40:08.320 align:middle line:84%
And so to prove this, there's
a nice mathematical trick.

00:40:08.320 --> 00:40:16.560 align:middle line:84%
And so if we look at this term
on the right-hand side here,

00:40:16.560 --> 00:40:21.760 align:middle line:84%
which is an element of the sum,
it turns out to be equal to 1/2

00:40:21.760 --> 00:40:24.960 align:middle line:84%
the difference in the squared
values at the endpoints

00:40:24.960 --> 00:40:32.360 align:middle line:84%
of the interval minus 1/2 times
the difference between ti and ti

00:40:32.360 --> 00:40:34.200 align:middle line:90%
plus 1 squared.

00:40:34.200 --> 00:40:38.120 align:middle line:90%
So this is simple to verify.

00:40:38.120 --> 00:40:43.000 align:middle line:84%
It's trivial but
incredibly useful.

00:40:43.000 --> 00:40:50.440 align:middle line:84%
And so if we sum over all the
time points from 0 to n minus 1,

00:40:50.440 --> 00:40:58.680 align:middle line:84%
we get the sum of the
first terms minus the sum

00:40:58.680 --> 00:41:01.680 align:middle line:90%
of the second terms.

00:41:01.680 --> 00:41:11.200 align:middle line:84%
And so this first term is
actually a telescoping sum.

00:41:11.200 --> 00:41:19.480 align:middle line:84%
When you sum the terms, there's
cancelation of the middle value

00:41:19.480 --> 00:41:21.400 align:middle line:90%
in successive terms.

00:41:21.400 --> 00:41:30.840 align:middle line:84%
And so we're left at the end
with 1/2 the square of the last

00:41:30.840 --> 00:41:35.400 align:middle line:84%
term here minus the square
of the very first term,

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:39.900 align:middle line:84%
with i equals 0
here, which is 0.

00:41:39.900 --> 00:41:44.400 align:middle line:84%
So this is 1/2 Bt squared
minus B0 squared, which is 0.

00:41:44.400 --> 00:41:46.200 align:middle line:90%
That's the first term.

00:41:46.200 --> 00:41:53.120 align:middle line:84%
And then for the other, we have
minus 1/2 the sum of the squared

00:41:53.120 --> 00:41:54.600 align:middle line:90%
increments.

00:41:54.600 --> 00:42:07.680 align:middle line:84%
And so if we know what the
last time interval term is, tn,

00:42:07.680 --> 00:42:11.680 align:middle line:84%
that's capital T.
And then we have

00:42:11.680 --> 00:42:14.080 align:middle line:90%
the same terms on the right.

00:42:14.080 --> 00:42:20.800 align:middle line:84%
And what's important to
know is that the limit

00:42:20.800 --> 00:42:28.440 align:middle line:84%
of the sums of these increments
converges to T-- capital T.

00:42:28.440 --> 00:42:33.520 align:middle line:84%
And this was the property
of the total variation

00:42:33.520 --> 00:42:36.440 align:middle line:84%
of Brownian motion
over any increment.

00:42:36.440 --> 00:42:41.440 align:middle line:84%
If we look at the sum of
squared values of increments

00:42:41.440 --> 00:42:45.600 align:middle line:84%
and take the limit as the
number of increments grows large

00:42:45.600 --> 00:42:51.680 align:middle line:84%
and the partition
gets finer and finer,

00:42:51.680 --> 00:42:56.040 align:middle line:84%
then the limiting value of
these sums of increments

00:42:56.040 --> 00:43:00.240 align:middle line:84%
is equal to capital T.
So this is our formula

00:43:00.240 --> 00:43:07.880 align:middle line:84%
now for the Ito
integral of B dBs.

00:43:07.880 --> 00:43:12.160 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

00:43:12.160 --> 00:43:17.000 align:middle line:84%
So what's really
important to highlight

00:43:17.000 --> 00:43:24.160 align:middle line:84%
is that this is not equivalent
to thinking of the Brownian

00:43:24.160 --> 00:43:28.360 align:middle line:84%
motion process like
a real function,

00:43:28.360 --> 00:43:35.640 align:middle line:84%
where we're integrating BdB or
xdx, which is x squared over 2.

00:43:35.640 --> 00:43:38.760 align:middle line:84%
We actually have this
drag on the integral

00:43:38.760 --> 00:43:43.720 align:middle line:84%
that depends on the
variation of the process.

00:43:43.720 --> 00:43:44.220 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:43:44.220 --> 00:43:51.590 align:middle line:84%
Now, we want to generalize
to the general case of Ito

00:43:51.590 --> 00:43:53.510 align:middle line:90%
integrals.

00:43:53.510 --> 00:44:05.590 align:middle line:84%
And so what we do is we consider
defining the Ito integrals

00:44:05.590 --> 00:44:11.880 align:middle line:90%
from 0 to T f of omega s dBs.

00:44:11.880 --> 00:44:15.270 align:middle line:90%


00:44:15.270 --> 00:44:23.110 align:middle line:84%
But then we want to
basically consider this

00:44:23.110 --> 00:44:26.600 align:middle line:90%
to be a process Xt of omega.

00:44:26.600 --> 00:44:29.310 align:middle line:90%


00:44:29.310 --> 00:44:37.110 align:middle line:84%
And we want to generalize
this to an Xt of omega,

00:44:37.110 --> 00:44:46.510 align:middle line:84%
where we're integrating
just up to time little t.

00:44:46.510 --> 00:44:54.470 align:middle line:84%
So what we want
to do is, once we

00:44:54.470 --> 00:45:01.550 align:middle line:84%
have this definition
for the Ito integral

00:45:01.550 --> 00:45:11.710 align:middle line:84%
over a fixed
interval from 0 to T,

00:45:11.710 --> 00:45:18.950 align:middle line:84%
we want to be able to define
the Ito integral where

00:45:18.950 --> 00:45:22.190 align:middle line:84%
we're integrating only
up to time little t

00:45:22.190 --> 00:45:25.390 align:middle line:90%
less than capital T.

00:45:25.390 --> 00:45:40.430 align:middle line:84%
And so in order for this kind
of function to be well defined,

00:45:40.430 --> 00:45:45.110 align:middle line:84%
it will turn out that
we need some very strong

00:45:45.110 --> 00:45:47.070 align:middle line:90%
mathematical arguments.

00:45:47.070 --> 00:45:50.910 align:middle line:84%
And there's a book
by Steele, chapter 6,

00:45:50.910 --> 00:45:57.710 align:middle line:84%
on quantitative finance, where
he does go into all the details.

00:45:57.710 --> 00:46:01.430 align:middle line:84%
And I have to admit, it's
not really a fun read.

00:46:01.430 --> 00:46:03.350 align:middle line:84%
It's interesting to
look and see what's

00:46:03.350 --> 00:46:05.910 align:middle line:84%
involved so you know
how important it

00:46:05.910 --> 00:46:09.630 align:middle line:84%
is to be a good mathematician,
if you want to prove things.

00:46:09.630 --> 00:46:12.470 align:middle line:84%
But fortunate for us,
they're proven well,

00:46:12.470 --> 00:46:14.590 align:middle line:90%
and we can apply them.

00:46:14.590 --> 00:46:15.610 align:middle line:90%
So what do we need?

00:46:15.610 --> 00:46:21.950 align:middle line:84%
We need that the function
little f needs to be measurable,

00:46:21.950 --> 00:46:24.590 align:middle line:84%
script Fs, the
information set up to time

00:46:24.590 --> 00:46:27.990 align:middle line:90%
s measurable for every time s.

00:46:27.990 --> 00:46:39.790 align:middle line:84%
And we want the integral
of the squared f over time,

00:46:39.790 --> 00:46:43.090 align:middle line:84%
where that's for
each fixed omega,

00:46:43.090 --> 00:46:46.500 align:middle line:84%
we can integrate the
square of f and then take

00:46:46.500 --> 00:46:49.620 align:middle line:90%
the expectation of that.

00:46:49.620 --> 00:46:53.660 align:middle line:90%
We want that to be finite.

00:46:53.660 --> 00:47:00.540 align:middle line:84%
So this term here,
this expectation,

00:47:00.540 --> 00:47:05.980 align:middle line:84%
is taking the expectation
over the probability model

00:47:05.980 --> 00:47:12.340 align:middle line:84%
given by capital P.
And for each omega,

00:47:12.340 --> 00:47:19.180 align:middle line:84%
we're integrating the squared
f over time from 0 to T.

00:47:19.180 --> 00:47:24.780 align:middle line:90%
And we want that to be finite.

00:47:24.780 --> 00:47:30.260 align:middle line:84%
So with this
notation, we basically

00:47:30.260 --> 00:47:38.340 align:middle line:84%
are extending our Ito
integral over 0 to capital T,

00:47:38.340 --> 00:47:42.740 align:middle line:84%
with the Ito integral
from 0 to little t.

00:47:42.740 --> 00:47:51.380 align:middle line:84%
And we can define this by
just replacing our f function

00:47:51.380 --> 00:48:00.060 align:middle line:84%
in the Ito integral I of
f by simply the function

00:48:00.060 --> 00:48:04.460 align:middle line:84%
f times this
indicator from 0 to T.

00:48:04.460 --> 00:48:13.460 align:middle line:84%
So that step basically chops off
the integration at time little t

00:48:13.460 --> 00:48:15.980 align:middle line:90%
and gives us our definition.

00:48:15.980 --> 00:48:20.780 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

00:48:20.780 --> 00:48:21.600 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:48:21.600 --> 00:48:30.100 align:middle line:84%
The definition of this extension
of our Ito integral over 0

00:48:30.100 --> 00:48:36.460 align:middle line:84%
to T to integrating it over 0
to little t's for any little t,

00:48:36.460 --> 00:48:43.100 align:middle line:84%
less than t, that's relatively
straightforward and intuitively

00:48:43.100 --> 00:48:44.340 align:middle line:90%
obvious.

00:48:44.340 --> 00:48:46.920 align:middle line:84%
What's not necessarily
obvious, though,

00:48:46.920 --> 00:48:57.340 align:middle line:84%
is that our process Xt is
a martingale with respect

00:48:57.340 --> 00:49:03.260 align:middle line:84%
to this stochastic process model
given by the Brownian motions.

00:49:03.260 --> 00:49:11.140 align:middle line:84%
So if we look at what
it means for a process

00:49:11.140 --> 00:49:16.020 align:middle line:84%
to be a martingale, if we look
at the conditional expectation

00:49:16.020 --> 00:49:22.420 align:middle line:84%
of the time t plus k
value given time t,

00:49:22.420 --> 00:49:27.660 align:middle line:84%
then it equals the value
of the process at time t,

00:49:27.660 --> 00:49:34.220 align:middle line:84%
little t, plus the integral
of the process from time t

00:49:34.220 --> 00:49:36.780 align:middle line:90%
to t plus k.

00:49:36.780 --> 00:49:39.860 align:middle line:84%
And we're taking expectations,
conditional expectations

00:49:39.860 --> 00:49:43.060 align:middle line:90%
of that given Xt.

00:49:43.060 --> 00:49:49.900 align:middle line:84%
And so the expectation
of this future increment

00:49:49.900 --> 00:49:55.100 align:middle line:84%
from t to t plus k is basically
integrating a function times

00:49:55.100 --> 00:49:56.680 align:middle line:84%
increments of the
Brownian motion,

00:49:56.680 --> 00:50:00.300 align:middle line:84%
where our function values
depend on the past,

00:50:00.300 --> 00:50:06.220 align:middle line:84%
and the increments are
independent in the future,

00:50:06.220 --> 00:50:07.360 align:middle line:90%
with mean 0.

00:50:07.360 --> 00:50:10.940 align:middle line:84%
So this expectation turns
out to be satisfied.

00:50:10.940 --> 00:50:14.020 align:middle line:84%
And if we look at the
conditional variance

00:50:14.020 --> 00:50:18.700 align:middle line:84%
of the t plus k value
given Xt, then it's

00:50:18.700 --> 00:50:27.700 align:middle line:84%
equal to the expected
square of this f ds.

00:50:27.700 --> 00:50:33.220 align:middle line:84%
And so when you look at the
variance of the discretized sum

00:50:33.220 --> 00:50:37.180 align:middle line:84%
of increments, you get
the discretized sum

00:50:37.180 --> 00:50:42.610 align:middle line:84%
of variances of those
increments, which are f squared.

00:50:42.610 --> 00:50:48.610 align:middle line:84%
And then the dBs
squareds are dt.

00:50:48.610 --> 00:50:53.140 align:middle line:84%
So this simplifies for
the variance to that term.

00:50:53.140 --> 00:50:56.250 align:middle line:90%


00:50:56.250 --> 00:50:56.750 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:50:56.750 --> 00:50:57.880 align:middle line:90%
All of this is fairly--

00:50:57.880 --> 00:51:00.610 align:middle line:90%


00:51:00.610 --> 00:51:02.550 align:middle line:84%
I know, it's like
mathematical results.

00:51:02.550 --> 00:51:04.280 align:middle line:84%
And it's sort of
like, who cares?

00:51:04.280 --> 00:51:06.850 align:middle line:90%


00:51:06.850 --> 00:51:15.930 align:middle line:84%
Well, it turns out that
this variance turns out

00:51:15.930 --> 00:51:20.530 align:middle line:84%
to be a squared norm
of our function little

00:51:20.530 --> 00:51:28.670 align:middle line:84%
f on the space of the
probability space of paths

00:51:28.670 --> 00:51:31.530 align:middle line:90%
cross the time increments.

00:51:31.530 --> 00:51:37.490 align:middle line:90%
And so what's-- well, OK.

00:51:37.490 --> 00:51:40.890 align:middle line:84%
Actually, I'm going to
get ahead of myself.

00:51:40.890 --> 00:51:50.930 align:middle line:84%
But let me get ahead of
myself and basically comment

00:51:50.930 --> 00:51:59.570 align:middle line:84%
that this norm of
our function f is

00:51:59.570 --> 00:52:05.090 align:middle line:84%
going to be a squared norm
on the space of Ito-process

00:52:05.090 --> 00:52:07.490 align:middle line:90%
integrands.

00:52:07.490 --> 00:52:09.990 align:middle line:90%
And, let's see.

00:52:09.990 --> 00:52:13.710 align:middle line:84%
Those of you who
have taken, I guess,

00:52:13.710 --> 00:52:19.410 align:middle line:84%
real analysis or
metric spaces, what's

00:52:19.410 --> 00:52:24.370 align:middle line:90%
an important value of norms?

00:52:24.370 --> 00:52:25.880 align:middle line:90%
What do we do with norms?

00:52:25.880 --> 00:52:28.570 align:middle line:90%


00:52:28.570 --> 00:52:29.680 align:middle line:90%
Or squared norms?

00:52:29.680 --> 00:52:34.490 align:middle line:90%


00:52:34.490 --> 00:52:39.650 align:middle line:84%
Well, with norms, we
can talk about limits

00:52:39.650 --> 00:52:47.890 align:middle line:84%
of elements of the space and
talk about whether limits exist.

00:52:47.890 --> 00:52:51.490 align:middle line:84%
And so with this
setup, we actually

00:52:51.490 --> 00:52:56.730 align:middle line:84%
will have the space
of Ito processes.

00:52:56.730 --> 00:53:04.770 align:middle line:84%
And we can calculate the squared
norm between different Ito

00:53:04.770 --> 00:53:07.210 align:middle line:90%
integrals.

00:53:07.210 --> 00:53:13.810 align:middle line:84%
And so we can characterize
limits in those spaces

00:53:13.810 --> 00:53:18.610 align:middle line:84%
and be guaranteed that
those limits exist.

00:53:18.610 --> 00:53:25.130 align:middle line:84%
So with this motivation
of defining useful norms

00:53:25.130 --> 00:53:31.250 align:middle line:84%
on different spaces, we
have this Ito isometry,

00:53:31.250 --> 00:53:38.640 align:middle line:84%
which is, if we look
at the variance of Ito

00:53:38.640 --> 00:53:44.040 align:middle line:84%
integrals defined by
integrand little f,

00:53:44.040 --> 00:53:50.400 align:middle line:84%
then those squared norms or the
variances, basically, of the Ito

00:53:50.400 --> 00:53:59.040 align:middle line:84%
processes is equal to the L2
norm of the integrand little f.

00:53:59.040 --> 00:54:03.800 align:middle line:84%
And if we write
out the definitions

00:54:03.800 --> 00:54:11.080 align:middle line:84%
of these squared norms,
we get these equations.

00:54:11.080 --> 00:54:18.960 align:middle line:84%
And what's relevant
here is being

00:54:18.960 --> 00:54:26.080 align:middle line:84%
able to express our
variance of the Ito integral

00:54:26.080 --> 00:54:31.180 align:middle line:84%
as the squared norm
of the integrand f.

00:54:31.180 --> 00:54:34.240 align:middle line:90%


00:54:34.240 --> 00:54:36.280 align:middle line:84%
So we have limits
and convergences

00:54:36.280 --> 00:54:38.880 align:middle line:90%
of sequences of Ito integrals.

00:54:38.880 --> 00:54:43.120 align:middle line:84%
And something that's
important mathematically

00:54:43.120 --> 00:54:49.400 align:middle line:84%
is, if we take the limits
of these Ito integrals

00:54:49.400 --> 00:54:51.720 align:middle line:84%
over increments
that are getting--

00:54:51.720 --> 00:54:54.520 align:middle line:84%
a grid of increments that's
getting finer and finer,

00:54:54.520 --> 00:54:58.320 align:middle line:84%
do we have a unique
limit or not?

00:54:58.320 --> 00:55:02.440 align:middle line:90%
And so that's what's important.

00:55:02.440 --> 00:55:08.600 align:middle line:84%
And the squared norms and
convergence properties

00:55:08.600 --> 00:55:11.280 align:middle line:90%
are used to prove that.

00:55:11.280 --> 00:55:17.000 align:middle line:84%
So we basically can talk
about closed linear subspaces

00:55:17.000 --> 00:55:23.960 align:middle line:90%
with these norms on the space.

00:55:23.960 --> 00:55:26.840 align:middle line:84%
So what this does
is it allows us

00:55:26.840 --> 00:55:32.560 align:middle line:84%
to define the distance
between different integrands

00:55:32.560 --> 00:55:48.040 align:middle line:84%
f and the existence of
a limiting Ito integral,

00:55:48.040 --> 00:55:53.640 align:middle line:84%
as we consider increments
getting infinitesimal.

00:55:53.640 --> 00:55:55.720 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:55:55.720 --> 00:56:00.680 align:middle line:84%
Well, we can think
of this Ito integral

00:56:00.680 --> 00:56:05.720 align:middle line:84%
as a Riemann sum, a
limiting Riemann sum.

00:56:05.720 --> 00:56:15.240 align:middle line:84%
And if we have time steps ti,
for simplicity, equal to iT

00:56:15.240 --> 00:56:22.680 align:middle line:84%
over n, we can consider
the limiting sum

00:56:22.680 --> 00:56:26.800 align:middle line:84%
of f of the Brownian
motion times the increment

00:56:26.800 --> 00:56:28.760 align:middle line:90%
of the Brownian motion.

00:56:28.760 --> 00:56:37.240 align:middle line:84%
And this sum actually
converges in probability,

00:56:37.240 --> 00:56:43.480 align:middle line:84%
as n goes to infinity,
to the integral.

00:56:43.480 --> 00:56:49.280 align:middle line:84%
So we have a limiting
value that exists.

00:56:49.280 --> 00:56:51.440 align:middle line:90%
Convergence is in probability.

00:56:51.440 --> 00:57:01.720 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider, I guess,
the covariances of the X process

00:57:01.720 --> 00:57:09.160 align:middle line:84%
given by these Ito integrals
from 0 to little t,

00:57:09.160 --> 00:57:12.640 align:middle line:90%
then these will--

00:57:12.640 --> 00:57:16.200 align:middle line:84%
or this Xt will be a
mean 0 Gaussian process

00:57:16.200 --> 00:57:18.480 align:middle line:90%
with independent increments.

00:57:18.480 --> 00:57:24.720 align:middle line:84%
And the covariance between the
process at time s and time t

00:57:24.720 --> 00:57:30.150 align:middle line:84%
will simply be equal to
the integral of the squared

00:57:30.150 --> 00:57:35.250 align:middle line:84%
f over the times where
they both are non-zero.

00:57:35.250 --> 00:57:40.350 align:middle line:90%


00:57:40.350 --> 00:57:40.850 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:57:40.850 --> 00:57:43.590 align:middle line:84%
So we take a
partition from 0 to t.

00:57:43.590 --> 00:57:51.470 align:middle line:84%
We get this result. And we can
prove that the limit equals

00:57:51.470 --> 00:57:53.750 align:middle line:90%
this integral notation.

00:57:53.750 --> 00:57:55.970 align:middle line:84%
So basically, there's
a limit that exists,

00:57:55.970 --> 00:57:59.510 align:middle line:84%
and this is a notation
for that limit.

00:57:59.510 --> 00:58:03.110 align:middle line:84%
And Steele goes into a lot
of the details for that.

00:58:03.110 --> 00:58:08.590 align:middle line:90%


00:58:08.590 --> 00:58:13.270 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's of interest
is that we can now

00:58:13.270 --> 00:58:18.590 align:middle line:84%
consider some general
Ito cases, which

00:58:18.590 --> 00:58:22.310 align:middle line:84%
will be referred to as Ito's
formula, for different kinds

00:58:22.310 --> 00:58:25.470 align:middle line:90%
of functions f.

00:58:25.470 --> 00:58:29.510 align:middle line:84%
And so if we consider
first the case

00:58:29.510 --> 00:58:36.470 align:middle line:84%
where f, little f, is a real
function mapping reals to reals,

00:58:36.470 --> 00:58:43.350 align:middle line:84%
then if it has a continuous
second derivative,

00:58:43.350 --> 00:58:53.910 align:middle line:84%
then we can express the function
f of Bt in terms of its Taylor

00:58:53.910 --> 00:58:59.430 align:middle line:84%
approximation, and we
get this formula, which

00:58:59.430 --> 00:59:03.470 align:middle line:90%
is Ito's formula for f of Bt.

00:59:03.470 --> 00:59:09.910 align:middle line:84%
And in looking at this
expression, what's relevant

00:59:09.910 --> 00:59:14.550 align:middle line:84%
is that, OK, the first
term, f0, is a constant.

00:59:14.550 --> 00:59:21.050 align:middle line:84%
The second term is a
Gaussian process integral.

00:59:21.050 --> 00:59:23.190 align:middle line:84%
It's an integral
of Brownian motion.

00:59:23.190 --> 00:59:26.910 align:middle line:84%
And then the third
term is the integral

00:59:26.910 --> 00:59:35.390 align:middle line:84%
of the second derivative of
f evaluated at the Bs value.

00:59:35.390 --> 00:59:39.470 align:middle line:84%
And so we have an
adaptive drift term.

00:59:39.470 --> 00:59:45.850 align:middle line:84%
And we can prove this formula,
or outline the proof by saying,

00:59:45.850 --> 00:59:49.710 align:middle line:84%
OK, let's consider the
Taylor approximation of f

00:59:49.710 --> 00:59:56.350 align:middle line:84%
at x plus delta x and expand
it to second-order terms

00:59:56.350 --> 00:59:58.790 align:middle line:90%
plus a remainder.

00:59:58.790 --> 01:00:09.150 align:middle line:84%
And then if we take the
difference of f at time t with B

01:00:09.150 --> 01:00:13.590 align:middle line:84%
minus f at time 0
with B, then we're

01:00:13.590 --> 01:00:23.540 align:middle line:84%
looking at the sum of the
increments from time t1 to tn.

01:00:23.540 --> 01:00:31.700 align:middle line:84%
And those increments are equal
to the sums of the derivative

01:00:31.700 --> 01:00:37.780 align:middle line:84%
of f times the increment of time
plus 1/2 the second derivative

01:00:37.780 --> 01:00:43.260 align:middle line:84%
of f times the squared increment
plus the remainder terms.

01:00:43.260 --> 01:00:46.100 align:middle line:84%
And if we take the
limits of those,

01:00:46.100 --> 01:00:53.340 align:middle line:84%
we get the integral of f prime
dBs plus 1/2 the integral

01:00:53.340 --> 01:00:55.060 align:middle line:90%
of the second derivative.

01:00:55.060 --> 01:00:58.740 align:middle line:84%
So Taylor series
gives us this formula.

01:00:58.740 --> 01:01:06.160 align:middle line:84%
And we can think of developing
a notation with Ito calculus,

01:01:06.160 --> 01:01:17.300 align:middle line:84%
which is that our differential
of f at Bs is equal to f prime

01:01:17.300 --> 01:01:26.180 align:middle line:84%
of Bs dBs plus 1/2 f double
prime times Bs times the squared

01:01:26.180 --> 01:01:33.860 align:middle line:84%
of dB, which is equal to
ds, the increment of time.

01:01:33.860 --> 01:01:38.300 align:middle line:84%
So this is our Ito's
formula shorthand.

01:01:38.300 --> 01:01:45.780 align:middle line:84%
And this shorthand
corresponds in a similar way

01:01:45.780 --> 01:01:51.420 align:middle line:84%
to derivatives and
antiderivatives, where here's

01:01:51.420 --> 01:01:54.660 align:middle line:84%
the stochastic
differential equation,

01:01:54.660 --> 01:01:58.980 align:middle line:84%
and here's the solution to
that stochastic differential

01:01:58.980 --> 01:02:02.820 align:middle line:90%
equation on the top.

01:02:02.820 --> 01:02:10.260 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's really neat
and very powerful is

01:02:10.260 --> 01:02:18.780 align:middle line:84%
that if we want to solve for
the integral of a function--

01:02:18.780 --> 01:02:23.740 align:middle line:84%
so suppose our objective is to
solve for the integral from 0

01:02:23.740 --> 01:02:28.150 align:middle line:90%
to T f of Bs dBs.

01:02:28.150 --> 01:02:31.180 align:middle line:90%


01:02:31.180 --> 01:02:40.380 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we have the
antiderivative of little f--

01:02:40.380 --> 01:02:44.980 align:middle line:84%
so that means that the first
derivative of capital F

01:02:44.980 --> 01:02:54.400 align:middle line:84%
is equal to such that
F prime is equal to f--

01:02:54.400 --> 01:02:57.420 align:middle line:90%


01:02:57.420 --> 01:03:04.780 align:middle line:84%
then, if we choose the
antiderivative with f of 0 equal

01:03:04.780 --> 01:03:14.940 align:middle line:84%
to 0, then we get F of Bt
minus F of B0 is equal to this

01:03:14.940 --> 01:03:21.380 align:middle line:84%
integral of the derivative of
f dBs plus 1/2 the integral

01:03:21.380 --> 01:03:23.880 align:middle line:84%
of the second
derivative of capital F,

01:03:23.880 --> 01:03:26.020 align:middle line:90%
which is simply f prime.

01:03:26.020 --> 01:03:34.660 align:middle line:84%
So this formula is what we
just derived for Ito's formula.

01:03:34.660 --> 01:03:44.020 align:middle line:84%
And if we solve for this
first term on the right,

01:03:44.020 --> 01:03:48.740 align:middle line:84%
then that's equal to F of
Bt minus 1/2 the integral

01:03:48.740 --> 01:03:53.500 align:middle line:84%
of the first derivative
of f prime of Bs ds.

01:03:53.500 --> 01:04:00.740 align:middle line:84%
So what's neat as a technique
in stochastic calculus is that

01:04:00.740 --> 01:04:11.220 align:middle line:84%
if we want to integrate a
function little f of Bs dBs,

01:04:11.220 --> 01:04:19.090 align:middle line:84%
then we can just apply
Ito's formula in reverse

01:04:19.090 --> 01:04:24.370 align:middle line:84%
and characterize it as the
antiderivative of little f at Bt

01:04:24.370 --> 01:04:28.410 align:middle line:84%
minus 1/2 that, defining the
antiderivative to have f of 0

01:04:28.410 --> 01:04:30.650 align:middle line:90%
equal to 0.

01:04:30.650 --> 01:04:37.010 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's apply this just for
the case where capital F of x

01:04:37.010 --> 01:04:41.330 align:middle line:84%
equals x, capital F prime,
the derivative is 1,

01:04:41.330 --> 01:04:49.330 align:middle line:84%
and the second derivative is
0, then the integral of dBs

01:04:49.330 --> 01:04:51.890 align:middle line:90%
is equal to Bt.

01:04:51.890 --> 01:04:56.250 align:middle line:90%
And let's see.

01:04:56.250 --> 01:05:07.010 align:middle line:84%
So basically, the integral of Bs
dBs is equal to capital F of Bt,

01:05:07.010 --> 01:05:13.490 align:middle line:84%
which is Bt, and then minus
1/2 the second derivative--

01:05:13.490 --> 01:05:17.970 align:middle line:84%
or the second derivative
of f, which is 0.

01:05:17.970 --> 01:05:21.970 align:middle line:90%
So this gives us this result.

01:05:21.970 --> 01:05:28.770 align:middle line:84%
And if we consider capital
F equaling 1/2 x squared,

01:05:28.770 --> 01:05:36.850 align:middle line:84%
then little f is equal to x, and
little f prime is equal to 1.

01:05:36.850 --> 01:05:41.950 align:middle line:84%
And so we get the integral of
Bs dBs is f of Bt minus that,

01:05:41.950 --> 01:05:44.970 align:middle line:84%
which is 1/2 Bt
squared minus 1/2 t.

01:05:44.970 --> 01:05:49.210 align:middle line:84%
So this gives us of a
verification of the limiting

01:05:49.210 --> 01:05:55.130 align:middle line:84%
results we went through before
with these Ito integrals.

01:05:55.130 --> 01:06:01.330 align:middle line:84%
The next step is to extend
Ito's formula to functions

01:06:01.330 --> 01:06:03.690 align:middle line:90%
of more than one variable.

01:06:03.690 --> 01:06:15.410 align:middle line:84%
So we basically want to go
from f of x, so one variable s.

01:06:15.410 --> 01:06:21.170 align:middle line:84%
We want to go to two
variables functions, f.

01:06:21.170 --> 01:06:24.810 align:middle line:84%
And we'll write the
notation of s comma

01:06:24.810 --> 01:06:37.690 align:middle line:84%
x, which maps, basically, let's
see, the time space cross--

01:06:37.690 --> 01:06:45.170 align:middle line:84%
or time range, R plus, cross the
real line of x into the reals.

01:06:45.170 --> 01:06:48.130 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to
consider functions little

01:06:48.130 --> 01:06:56.210 align:middle line:84%
f that are continuous to
different orders in terms

01:06:56.210 --> 01:07:00.850 align:middle line:84%
of the time variable
and the space variable.

01:07:00.850 --> 01:07:08.810 align:middle line:84%
So we have this notation,
C super 1 comma 2,

01:07:08.810 --> 01:07:16.530 align:middle line:84%
meaning that it's continuous
with a first-order derivative,

01:07:16.530 --> 01:07:20.970 align:middle line:84%
existing and continuous
and second-order derivative

01:07:20.970 --> 01:07:24.610 align:middle line:84%
with respect to
x and continuous.

01:07:24.610 --> 01:07:29.450 align:middle line:84%
And so we have a Taylor
series for increments

01:07:29.450 --> 01:07:34.250 align:middle line:84%
of this function little f,
now, of two variables, which

01:07:34.250 --> 01:07:38.730 align:middle line:84%
has the multidimensional
d equals

01:07:38.730 --> 01:07:42.130 align:middle line:90%
2 Taylor series formulation.

01:07:42.130 --> 01:07:49.890 align:middle line:84%
And it equals the f
without the increments on t

01:07:49.890 --> 01:07:54.570 align:middle line:84%
and x plus delta t times the
partial of f with respect

01:07:54.570 --> 01:07:59.130 align:middle line:84%
to t plus delta x times the
partial of f with respect to x.

01:07:59.130 --> 01:08:03.370 align:middle line:84%
And then we have the
second-order partial

01:08:03.370 --> 01:08:05.550 align:middle line:90%
differential terms.

01:08:05.550 --> 01:08:10.290 align:middle line:84%
And the only ones that are
going to be of interest

01:08:10.290 --> 01:08:14.480 align:middle line:84%
are the second-order
partial of f

01:08:14.480 --> 01:08:20.120 align:middle line:84%
with respect to x, which will be
multiplied by delta x squared.

01:08:20.120 --> 01:08:22.760 align:middle line:84%
The other second-order
terms will actually

01:08:22.760 --> 01:08:27.389 align:middle line:90%
be of small order delta t.

01:08:27.389 --> 01:08:34.680 align:middle line:84%
And with this second-order
Taylor approximation,

01:08:34.680 --> 01:08:39.399 align:middle line:84%
we're able to derive
that the function f of t,

01:08:39.399 --> 01:08:48.120 align:middle line:84%
Bt is equal to the initial
value plus an integral over time

01:08:48.120 --> 01:08:54.680 align:middle line:84%
of a function of the Brownian
motion ds plus an integral over

01:08:54.680 --> 01:09:01.120 align:middle line:84%
increments of the Brownian
motion plus 1/2 this integral

01:09:01.120 --> 01:09:04.520 align:middle line:84%
of the second partial of f
with respect to dx squared.

01:09:04.520 --> 01:09:11.960 align:middle line:84%
And this integral here ends
up being multiplied-- or being

01:09:11.960 --> 01:09:14.380 align:middle line:84%
integrated with respect
to the increments of time,

01:09:14.380 --> 01:09:21.640 align:middle line:84%
because the delta x-squared
terms are infinitesimally

01:09:21.640 --> 01:09:23.669 align:middle line:90%
equivalent to increments ds.

01:09:23.669 --> 01:09:27.359 align:middle line:90%


01:09:27.359 --> 01:09:35.300 align:middle line:84%
So for this formula,
we have here,

01:09:35.300 --> 01:09:38.840 align:middle line:84%
if we look at the difference in
the little f function from time

01:09:38.840 --> 01:09:42.779 align:middle line:84%
0 to T, it equals
the right-hand side.

01:09:42.779 --> 01:09:45.640 align:middle line:84%
So this top formula
here is simply

01:09:45.640 --> 01:09:50.040 align:middle line:84%
taking this second-order
Taylor series

01:09:50.040 --> 01:09:53.800 align:middle line:84%
and looking at the
increment from 0

01:09:53.800 --> 01:09:58.000 align:middle line:90%
to T and what that equals.

01:09:58.000 --> 01:10:00.700 align:middle line:84%
And what we get is
a sum of two terms,

01:10:00.700 --> 01:10:06.160 align:middle line:84%
an integral with respect to
time of the partial with respect

01:10:06.160 --> 01:10:10.640 align:middle line:84%
to time plus 1/2 the second
derivative of f with respect

01:10:10.640 --> 01:10:11.560 align:middle line:90%
to x.

01:10:11.560 --> 01:10:20.440 align:middle line:84%
And with this notation
for the value of little

01:10:20.440 --> 01:10:26.960 align:middle line:84%
f, what's important
to notice is that,

01:10:26.960 --> 01:10:33.080 align:middle line:84%
if the argument in
the time integral--

01:10:33.080 --> 01:10:43.960 align:middle line:84%
if that's equal to 0, then
this will be a martingale

01:10:43.960 --> 01:10:51.640 align:middle line:84%
because the second term
is an integral of terms

01:10:51.640 --> 01:10:54.560 align:middle line:84%
times increments of
the Brownian motion.

01:10:54.560 --> 01:10:56.560 align:middle line:84%
And the increments of
the Brownian motion

01:10:56.560 --> 01:10:59.600 align:middle line:90%
all have expectation equal to 0.

01:10:59.600 --> 01:11:03.760 align:middle line:84%
So we'll have the
possibility of this function

01:11:03.760 --> 01:11:06.350 align:middle line:84%
being a martingale,
if that's true.

01:11:06.350 --> 01:11:12.070 align:middle line:84%
And this integrand
equaling 0 is equivalent

01:11:12.070 --> 01:11:17.950 align:middle line:84%
to this partial differential
equation on the little f.

01:11:17.950 --> 01:11:24.750 align:middle line:84%
So there's this connection
between martingales

01:11:24.750 --> 01:11:31.710 align:middle line:84%
and partial differential
equations for f when we're

01:11:31.710 --> 01:11:35.070 align:middle line:90%
thinking of a process f of t.

01:11:35.070 --> 01:11:42.430 align:middle line:84%
So if little f is a martingale,
it will turn out that

01:11:42.430 --> 01:11:48.670 align:middle line:84%
the partial of f with respect to
t will equal minus 1/2 d squared

01:11:48.670 --> 01:11:51.590 align:middle line:84%
f dx squared, which
is a PDE condition.

01:11:51.590 --> 01:11:56.950 align:middle line:84%
Now the other thing we want
actually for this to exist

01:11:56.950 --> 01:12:06.910 align:middle line:84%
is that the variance of
the function, f of T, BT,

01:12:06.910 --> 01:12:16.270 align:middle line:84%
which is bounded by the
squared expectation, is finite.

01:12:16.270 --> 01:12:22.350 align:middle line:84%
And so this will be
finite, the variance,

01:12:22.350 --> 01:12:29.110 align:middle line:84%
if that second term
has finite variance.

01:12:29.110 --> 01:12:32.710 align:middle line:84%
And so for that to
have finite variance,

01:12:32.710 --> 01:12:36.700 align:middle line:84%
the variance is the integral of
the square of that argument df

01:12:36.700 --> 01:12:39.160 align:middle line:90%
dx, with respect to time.

01:12:39.160 --> 01:12:45.190 align:middle line:90%


01:12:45.190 --> 01:12:51.870 align:middle line:84%
Now, what's interesting
with this Ito formula is

01:12:51.870 --> 01:12:57.230 align:middle line:84%
we can apply it to a
function little f, which

01:12:57.230 --> 01:13:03.870 align:middle line:84%
is the exponential of sigma x
minus sigma squared t over 2.

01:13:03.870 --> 01:13:09.950 align:middle line:84%
And importantly--
well, let's see.

01:13:09.950 --> 01:13:14.070 align:middle line:84%
This partial of f with respect
to t does equal minus 1/2 sigma

01:13:14.070 --> 01:13:16.070 align:middle line:90%
squared f.

01:13:16.070 --> 01:13:23.070 align:middle line:84%
And so this is actually a
partial differential equation

01:13:23.070 --> 01:13:26.530 align:middle line:90%
for little f.

01:13:26.530 --> 01:13:30.550 align:middle line:90%
But df dx is equal to sigma f.

01:13:30.550 --> 01:13:34.710 align:middle line:84%
The second partial of f with
respect to x is sigma-squared f.

01:13:34.710 --> 01:13:39.910 align:middle line:84%
And Ito's formula,
if we expand it out,

01:13:39.910 --> 01:13:43.742 align:middle line:84%
gives us this
expression for little f.

01:13:43.742 --> 01:13:53.190 align:middle line:84%
And this function little f
is, in fact, a martingale.

01:13:53.190 --> 01:14:03.830 align:middle line:84%
If we look at the expected
value of f given f of 0, 0,

01:14:03.830 --> 01:14:05.710 align:middle line:90%
it equals 1.

01:14:05.710 --> 01:14:12.510 align:middle line:84%
Now, f of 0, 0 is the
exponential of, basically,

01:14:12.510 --> 01:14:14.370 align:middle line:90%
0, which is 1.

01:14:14.370 --> 01:14:16.950 align:middle line:90%


01:14:16.950 --> 01:14:24.990 align:middle line:84%
But this function f, little
f, if we plug in Bt for x,

01:14:24.990 --> 01:14:30.150 align:middle line:84%
it's a scaled lognormal
random variable.

01:14:30.150 --> 01:14:36.210 align:middle line:84%
So this little f evaluated
at x equal to Bt--

01:14:36.210 --> 01:14:40.910 align:middle line:84%
so it's a transformation of
the Brownian motion process

01:14:40.910 --> 01:14:45.070 align:middle line:90%
paths, Bt by this function--

01:14:45.070 --> 01:14:50.310 align:middle line:84%
is actually a scaled
lognormal random variable.

01:14:50.310 --> 01:14:55.230 align:middle line:84%
And so e to the sigma Bt minus
1/2 sigma-squared t is a scaled

01:14:55.230 --> 01:14:56.670 align:middle line:90%
lognormal.

01:14:56.670 --> 01:15:01.860 align:middle line:84%
And the expected value of this
e to the sigma Bt is actually

01:15:01.860 --> 01:15:04.420 align:middle line:84%
equal to e to the
1/2 sigma-squared t.

01:15:04.420 --> 01:15:07.660 align:middle line:84%
We proved that, I believe,
in the problem set.

01:15:07.660 --> 01:15:09.940 align:middle line:84%
And so the
expectation of f of t,

01:15:09.940 --> 01:15:13.760 align:middle line:84%
Bt is going to actually
equal 1 for all t.

01:15:13.760 --> 01:15:21.580 align:middle line:84%
So this little f is,
in fact, a martingale.

01:15:21.580 --> 01:15:24.380 align:middle line:90%
Now, let's see.

01:15:24.380 --> 01:15:31.140 align:middle line:84%
With Brownian motion with
drift, the ruin problem,

01:15:31.140 --> 01:15:41.020 align:middle line:84%
we can actually use
some Ito-process ideas

01:15:41.020 --> 01:15:46.900 align:middle line:84%
to deal with this
problem, where we assume

01:15:46.900 --> 01:15:51.700 align:middle line:84%
that Xt is a Brownian
motion with drift mu

01:15:51.700 --> 01:15:54.780 align:middle line:84%
and variance parameter
sigma squared.

01:15:54.780 --> 01:16:00.300 align:middle line:84%
So here's its formula,
stochastic differential equation

01:16:00.300 --> 01:16:02.300 align:middle line:90%
for Xt.

01:16:02.300 --> 01:16:08.220 align:middle line:84%
And we're interested in, what's
the probability of stopping

01:16:08.220 --> 01:16:14.580 align:middle line:84%
at A before B, where
the stopping time

01:16:14.580 --> 01:16:21.820 align:middle line:84%
tau is the first time t
that hits A or minus B.

01:16:21.820 --> 01:16:28.340 align:middle line:84%
And this argument is
one that's very typical

01:16:28.340 --> 01:16:34.140 align:middle line:84%
of quantitative finance
developments, I guess,

01:16:34.140 --> 01:16:37.140 align:middle line:90%
by mathematicians.

01:16:37.140 --> 01:16:41.060 align:middle line:84%
And it's actually a
very neat argument,

01:16:41.060 --> 01:16:45.300 align:middle line:84%
where I don't think I could
ever have derived this,

01:16:45.300 --> 01:16:48.500 align:middle line:84%
but the derivation
using martingale theory

01:16:48.500 --> 01:16:50.760 align:middle line:90%
is really very elegant.

01:16:50.760 --> 01:16:58.540 align:middle line:84%
And so let's consider trying
to solve for this probability

01:16:58.540 --> 01:17:01.960 align:middle line:90%
by using martingale theory.

01:17:01.960 --> 01:17:07.540 align:middle line:90%
So let's consider a martingale.

01:17:07.540 --> 01:17:16.100 align:middle line:84%
Let's try to define a martingale
with value h of Xt defining

01:17:16.100 --> 01:17:26.500 align:middle line:84%
Mt, where the tau is
the same stopping time,

01:17:26.500 --> 01:17:31.500 align:middle line:84%
and the condition
on h is going to be

01:17:31.500 --> 01:17:38.060 align:middle line:84%
that if we are at the
value Xt equals A,

01:17:38.060 --> 01:17:41.620 align:middle line:90%
then the probability is 1.

01:17:41.620 --> 01:17:46.060 align:middle line:84%
And if we start at minus
B, the probability is 0.

01:17:46.060 --> 01:17:49.460 align:middle line:84%
So this is fixing the
terminal conditions

01:17:49.460 --> 01:17:57.290 align:middle line:84%
of the function h, when it
hits the limits A or minus B.

01:17:57.290 --> 01:18:06.690 align:middle line:84%
Well, if we have a martingale
that satisfies this function

01:18:06.690 --> 01:18:13.050 align:middle line:84%
h of Xt, then the expectation
of the stopped martingale

01:18:13.050 --> 01:18:20.410 align:middle line:84%
is simply equal to the
probability that X tau equals A.

01:18:20.410 --> 01:18:31.810 align:middle line:84%
And by the martingale
property, the expectation

01:18:31.810 --> 01:18:40.330 align:middle line:84%
of the stopped time is equal to
M0, the initial value of the h

01:18:40.330 --> 01:18:40.830 align:middle line:90%
function.

01:18:40.830 --> 01:18:46.510 align:middle line:84%
Now, the initial value is the
value when X is equal to 0.

01:18:46.510 --> 01:18:49.410 align:middle line:84%
So we're starting at
0 with the X process,

01:18:49.410 --> 01:18:56.210 align:middle line:84%
and it either moves and
hits A or hits minus B.

01:18:56.210 --> 01:19:04.970 align:middle line:84%
So in order for this h
function to be a martingale,

01:19:04.970 --> 01:19:10.890 align:middle line:84%
we have to have the PDE
condition satisfied on h.

01:19:10.890 --> 01:19:14.310 align:middle line:84%
So the partial of h with respect
to t is equal to minus 1/2

01:19:14.310 --> 01:19:18.010 align:middle line:84%
the second partial of
h with respect to dx.

01:19:18.010 --> 01:19:23.330 align:middle line:84%
And so if this h function, which
is the probability of hitting

01:19:23.330 --> 01:19:31.970 align:middle line:84%
A first, depends just
on the value of Xt,

01:19:31.970 --> 01:19:37.250 align:middle line:84%
then it equals h of
mu t plus sigma Bt,

01:19:37.250 --> 01:19:40.090 align:middle line:90%
substituting in for what Xt is.

01:19:40.090 --> 01:19:46.370 align:middle line:84%
So we can work with
the function little f

01:19:46.370 --> 01:19:50.850 align:middle line:90%
equal to h of mu t plus sigma x.

01:19:50.850 --> 01:19:56.250 align:middle line:84%
And if the partial of
f with respect to t,

01:19:56.250 --> 01:20:02.370 align:middle line:84%
which is, by chain rule, mu h
prime of mu t plus sigma x--

01:20:02.370 --> 01:20:04.930 align:middle line:84%
if that equals the second
partial of f with respect

01:20:04.930 --> 01:20:09.570 align:middle line:84%
to x, the second partial
derivative of h with respect

01:20:09.570 --> 01:20:13.010 align:middle line:84%
to x is sigma squared
h double prime,

01:20:13.010 --> 01:20:17.050 align:middle line:84%
then this PDE
condition says we have

01:20:17.050 --> 01:20:22.290 align:middle line:84%
this relationship, this
differential equation, for h.

01:20:22.290 --> 01:20:26.650 align:middle line:84%
Although, it's in the
first derivative of h.

01:20:26.650 --> 01:20:32.330 align:middle line:84%
But we can then rewrite this
as the second derivative

01:20:32.330 --> 01:20:38.170 align:middle line:84%
of h evaluated at argument
y is minus a constant times

01:20:38.170 --> 01:20:42.210 align:middle line:90%
the first derivative of h.

01:20:42.210 --> 01:20:47.410 align:middle line:84%
And so we can
solve that equation

01:20:47.410 --> 01:20:55.970 align:middle line:84%
by noting that h double
prime over h prime

01:20:55.970 --> 01:21:02.500 align:middle line:84%
is equal to d by dx
of log of h prime.

01:21:02.500 --> 01:21:07.170 align:middle line:90%


01:21:07.170 --> 01:21:11.810 align:middle line:84%
So the derivative of the log
of h prime is a constant.

01:21:11.810 --> 01:21:14.820 align:middle line:84%
The solution to that
is a linear function.

01:21:14.820 --> 01:21:17.410 align:middle line:90%


01:21:17.410 --> 01:21:22.210 align:middle line:84%
And so we then end up being
able to solve for little h of y

01:21:22.210 --> 01:21:24.050 align:middle line:90%
equaling this formula.

01:21:24.050 --> 01:21:28.550 align:middle line:84%
So this is the solution
to this problem.

01:21:28.550 --> 01:21:29.050 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:21:29.050 --> 01:21:29.590 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

01:21:29.590 --> 01:21:32.270 align:middle line:84%
We're at the end of
our lecture today.

01:21:32.270 --> 01:21:36.810 align:middle line:84%
So next time, we'll
finish up these notes

01:21:36.810 --> 01:21:44.370 align:middle line:84%
talking about standard
processes, where

01:21:44.370 --> 01:21:49.560 align:middle line:84%
our a function for the
drift and our b function

01:21:49.560 --> 01:21:54.520 align:middle line:84%
for the volatilities have to
satisfy certain properties.

01:21:54.520 --> 01:21:57.880 align:middle line:84%
And if you read
ahead in these notes,

01:21:57.880 --> 01:22:02.440 align:middle line:84%
you'll see that we need the
probability of integrating

01:22:02.440 --> 01:22:08.200 align:middle line:84%
the drift over time has to be
finite and absolute integral.

01:22:08.200 --> 01:22:14.640 align:middle line:84%
And the integral of the squared
b's have to also be finite.

01:22:14.640 --> 01:22:16.800 align:middle line:84%
If those were not
finite, then you

01:22:16.800 --> 01:22:22.760 align:middle line:84%
would, say, have explosive
behavior of these integrals,

01:22:22.760 --> 01:22:24.480 align:middle line:90%
where they won't exist.

01:22:24.480 --> 01:22:28.320 align:middle line:84%
So these are reasonable
properties to assume for those.

01:22:28.320 --> 01:22:32.040 align:middle line:84%
And anyway, we'll go through
some interesting results

01:22:32.040 --> 01:22:35.080 align:middle line:84%
here with this
particular problem

01:22:35.080 --> 01:22:38.160 align:middle line:84%
and then turn on to stochastic
differential equations

01:22:38.160 --> 01:22:39.760 align:middle line:90%
next time.

01:22:39.760 --> 01:22:41.400 align:middle line:90%
All right.

01:22:41.400 --> 01:22:43.270 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

01:22:43.270 --> 01:22:49.000 align:middle line:90%